WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Your goal: initialize and run Icepack
Run a synthetic shelf from your own gridded fields, then save the initial/final maps and native results.
Download run.py, initial_fields.py and icepack-shelf-initial.h5 from this tutorial page.
Start with your approved cloud account already connected and its cloud resources prepared.

2
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:27.000
1. Open the cloud workspace
Choose Cloud → Advanced → Icepack.
You will create a separate workspace example for this HDF5 run.
Keep the three supplied tutorial files together on your computer.

3
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:39.000
2. Create your own example
Enter a new name, then choose New example.
The new example has an empty file list, ready for your Python files.
The recorded example is pilot-icepack-h5-1118.

4
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:51.000
3. Select both Python files
Choose script files. In your file picker, select run.py and initial_fields.py, then choose Open.
Check that the button now shows two selected files.
You will import both files into the same workspace example.

5
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.000
4. Import the selected scripts
Choose Import script files.
If your example already contains run.py, enable Replace existing script files first.

6
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:06.000
4. Check both saved filenames
Wait for Imported run.py, initial_fields.py. Files are saved.
The file selector now contains run.py; the import confirmation lists both imported files.
For seeded or existing files, use Replace existing script files before importing.

7
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:14.000
5. Use run.py as the entry point
Set Run target to run.py.
This script calls initial_fields.py to validate and read your HDF5 input.
Importing files saves them; launching is a separate step.

8
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:26.000
6. Choose your HDF5 input
In Datasets choose Choose data files.
In your file picker select icepack-shelf-initial.h5 and choose Open. Check that one file is selected.
Next, upload the selected file into your workspace.

9
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:38.000
7. Upload the selected data file
Choose Upload. Wait for Uploaded: icepack-shelf-initial.h5.
Check that the new HDF5 filename appears in your dataset list.
The supplied file contains full thickness, base, surface, vx, vy and floating-mask grids.

10
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
8. Reference the file in this example
Select icepack-shelf-initial.h5. Keep that filename as the reference alias, then choose Reference in example.
Your script will read data/icepack-shelf-initial.h5.

11
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:54.000
8. Confirm the staged data path
Read the reference confirmation: data/icepack-shelf-initial.h5.
When you launch, CryoLauncher copies the selected dataset into that path for run.py to read.
The reference is attached to your current workspace example.

12
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:02.000
9. Review your configuration
Choose Review & Launch.
Before submitting, check that the summary names your intended model and workspace example.
Review the current settings before you launch.

13
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.000
9. Check the HDF5 run summary
Check Model: ICEPACK and Example: pilot-icepack-h5-1118.
Keep run.py as the entry point, with the referenced HDF5 input attached.
This native run used 2 vCPU and 8 GiB.

14
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.000
9. Launch when the checks are ready
Choose Launch cloud run when the readiness checks are ready.
Control guide; the original accepted HDF5 run follows.

15
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:22.000
9. Follow the new run card
Your HDF5 run now has its own Cloud Run card.
Wait for the job to finish; opening another tab does not cancel it.
This run used 2 vCPU and 8 GiB. Queue/startup and model timings are reported separately.

16
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.000
10. Open the completed results
When the run shows Completed, open View results.
The native script performs one 0.01-year step with zero accumulation.
Next, render and save the fields from this completed HDF5 run.

17
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.000
11. Render a native field
Choose Render to draw the selected field.
You can switch between thickness and velocity after the run finishes.

18
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:43.000
11. Render a native field
In Results choose solution icepack, choose velocity or thickness, then choose Render.
Use the field name and units to interpret the plot.
Velocity is in metres/year; thickness is in metres.

19
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:57.000
12. Inspect the initialized fields
Open initial-conditions.png in your saved results.
It shows thickness, surface and speed sampled from your uploaded grid onto the native mesh.
The supplied grid has 5 × 9 points and covers a 10 km × 10 km synthetic shelf.

20
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:13.000
13. Inspect the final thickness
Open the final thickness figure and compare the colour scale with the initial map.
Final thickness is 300.022696–499.980847 m; its area-weighted mean is 403.649861 m.
The final domain RMS speed is 133.908461 m/year.

21
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
14. Generate your download link
Choose Download results.
Wait for a new HTTPS download link for the current run.
Use the freshly generated link to save the ZIP.

22
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:25.000
14. Keep the result archive
Open the generated HTTPS link to save your results ZIP.
It contains the mesh, native fields, initial/final figures, metrics and runtime/RNG provenance.
Keep this 12-member archive and its manifest with your input files.

23
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:45.000
What this completed example establishes
The five initialized fields matched an independent interpolation at all 25 native nodes; the mesh has 32 triangles.
These portable CG1 fields are numeric results, rather than a Firedrake checkpoint. This example is not an independent native repeat or an accuracy assessment.
Submission → Batch start: 214.703 s; task: 46.352 s; script: 16.9818 s. Long waits are shortened in the video.
